\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
\] For the ambulance, the distance is 20 miles and the speed is 40 mph. The time taken by the ambulance is: \[
\text{Time}_{\text{ambulance}} = \frac{20 \text{ miles}}{40 \text{ mph}} = 0.5 \text{ hours}
\] Since 1 hour is equal to 60 minutes, the time taken by the ambulance in minutes is: \[
\text{Time}_{\text{ambulance}} = 0.5 \times 60 = 30 \text{ minutes}
\] Next, we need to calculate the total time taken by the helicopter to travel the 15 miles, including the takeoff, flight, and landing time. The time taken for the flight itself is: \[
\text{Time}_{\text{flight}} = \frac{15 \text{ miles}}{1 \text{ mile/minute}} = 15 \text{ minutes}
\] The total time taken by the helicopter is the sum of the takeoff time, flight time, and landing time: \[
\text{Time}_{\text{helicopter}} = 3 \text{ minutes (takeoff)} + 15 \text{ minutes (flight)} + 3 \text{ minutes (landing)} = 21 \text{ minutes}
\] The difference in time between the helicopter and the ambulance is: \[
\text{Difference} = \text{Time}_{\text{ambulance}} - \text{Time}_{\text{helicopter}} = 30 \text{ minutes} - 21 \text{ minutes} = 9 \text{ minutes}
\] Therefore, it takes 9 fewer minutes for the helicopter to complete its trip than for the ambulance to complete its trip. </think> <answer> 9 </answer>

